\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{quadratic-forms}

\taxon{Definition}
\title{Full adelic groups and the real factor}

\p{Let #{Q} be a nondegenerate quadratic form on a finite-dimensional
rational vector space #{V}, and choose a
\vocabk{compatible compact-open reference tuple}{fcap-004C}.
Extend scalars to #{V_\infty=\mathbb R\otimes_{\mathbb Q}V}, with
quadratic form #{Q_\infty}. Write #{O_\infty}, #{SO_\infty} and
#{\Spin_\infty} for its real groups with the canonical topologies
of \ref{fcap-0044}. In particular #{\Spin_\infty} uses the actual
vector-generated, even, reverse-norm-one carrier of \ref{fcap-0045}.}

\p{The \newvocab{full adelic orthogonal, special orthogonal and Spin groups}
relative to the chosen references are

##{\begin{aligned}
O_{\mathbb A}(U)&=O_\infty\times O_f(U),\\
SO_{\mathbb A}(U)&=SO_\infty\times SO_f(U),\\
\Spin_{\mathbb A}(U)&=\Spin_\infty\times\Spin_f(U).
\end{aligned}}

The finite factors are those of \ref{fcap-004F}. In each case take
the ordinary product topology and coordinatewise group operations.
There is no reference-subgroup condition at the real place: its
factor is unrestricted. More generally write
#{G_{\mathbb A}(U)=G_\infty\times G_f(U)} for any of these three
constructions. They are Hausdorff locally compact topological groups.}

\p{Indeed, \ref{fcap-0045} gives those properties for the real
factor, and \ref{fcap-004F} gives them for the finite factor.
Multiplication and inversion are continuous in each component,
hence in the product topology. Products of separating neighborhoods
give the Hausdorff property. At each point choose compact
neighborhoods in the two factors; their product is a compact
neighborhood, proving local compactness. The finite-coordinate
projection is the continuous group homomorphism

##{\begin{aligned}
\pi_f:G_{\mathbb A}(U)&\longrightarrow G_f(U),\\
(g_\infty,g_f)&\longmapsto g_f.
\end{aligned}}

Continuity is the defining property of the product topology, and
the homomorphism identity follows from coordinatewise multiplication.}

\p{For example, take the hyperbolic plane #{Q(x,y)=xy}. For #{t>0},
the real matrix #{h_t=\operatorname{diag}(t,t^{-1})} preserves #{Q}
and has determinant one. Thus #{(h_t,1)} is a point of
#{SO_{\mathbb A}(U)} for every #{t>0}, with identity at every
finite prime. The nonidentity matrices #{h_t} approach the identity
as #{t\to1}, exhibiting a nondiscrete real factor.}

\p{Let #{K(U)=\prod_pU_p} be the compact open reference subgroup
of the finite factor. The subgroup #{\{1\}\times K(U)} of the
full group is compact, but is not open when #{G_\infty} is
nondiscrete. Compactness follows from compactness of both factors.
If the subgroup were open, its inverse image under the continuous
map #{g\mapsto(g,1)} would make #{\{1\}} open in #{G_\infty}.
Translations would then make every singleton open, contradicting
nondiscreteness. Instead, for each open identity neighborhood
#{W_\infty\subseteq G_\infty}, the set
#{W_\infty\times K(U)} is an open identity neighborhood in the
full group. A compact real identity neighborhood
#{C_\infty} gives the compact identity neighborhood
#{C_\infty\times K(U)}, without asserting that it is open.
In dimension zero all three real and finite groups are singletons,
so the nondiscreteness qualification is essential.}

\p{The unrestricted archimedean factors and the finite-place
conditions occur together in the classical affine adelic stage
formula of \citet{Theorem 3.6, equation (3.6.1), pp. 6-8}{conrad2011adelic}.
Here the product construction is explicit for the chosen group
references; it makes no additional identification with integral
points of an affine scheme. TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.RestrictedProductGroupWithFactor}
is the general carrier with an extra unrestricted factor.
The three specializations are
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.fullAdelicOrthogonal},
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.fullAdelicSpecialOrthogonal}
and \leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.fullAdelicSpin}.
The corresponding continuous finite projections are
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.continuous_fullAdelicOrthogonalToFinite},
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.continuous_fullAdelicSpecialOrthogonalToFinite}
and \leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.continuous_fullAdelicSpinToFinite}.}
